rs to solve on  behalf the problem that is so massive  and severe but anyway that was the goal  and then there was annual reporting on  the Millennium Development Goals and  what they have come up with in the end  is that as far as extreme poverty is  concerned we have way over fulfilled the  target basically achieved the two-thirds  reduction and with regard to hunger we  have achieved roughly a 45 percent  reduction in the proportion where we  should have achieved a 50 percent  reduction but it's very crucial here to  look behind the numbers and how they  were generated okay let's let's do that  tell us about the numbers yeah so let me  start with hunger and what happened with  the hunger goal is that initially in the  first many years of the Millennium  Development Goal period the hunger  numbers were actually going up in  absolute terms and there was the famous  incident then in the year 2009 when the  FAO which was in charge of keeping the  numbers announced that for the first  time in all of human history more than a  billion people were chronically  undernourished it was one point zero to  three or twenty three million more than  a billion and they started the campaign  saying we are mad as hell more than a  billion people hungry now that was in  the wake of food riots in about 28  countries developing countries you may  remember the Arab Spring was partly  caused by these or initiated by these  food riots and it was very unwelcome  news  for the World Bank because the World  Bank had for years and years been saying  that extreme poverty is falling like a  brick much faster than we had even  announced and so the few people in the  world who care about these things they  were looking at these numbers and saying  that can't be right how can hunger go up  and up and up  while extreme poverty is going down and  down and down we thought that hunger is  extreme poverty these are essentially  the same people and so what happened  then was that very quickly  the FAO 