e inversions where they buy a company  abroad and then shift their headquarters  to the company they've bought so Pfizer  is currently doing that a big  pharmaceutical company trying to become  a non-us company and thereby permanently  to avoid paying taxes on the accumulated  money in tax havens so that's a problem  that the SDGs at least flag they mention  it they don't FEMA ties it in an  appropriate way but at least it's in  there and so and then of course the  environment the name sustainable  development goals already suggests that  there is a concern with the environment  and at least it gets mentioned ok so  ultimately what do you hope your book  will do the book I hope it will educate  citizens because we as citizens have a  responsibility to think about what is  being done in our names right we live in  a democratic society our government acts  in the name of the people of the country  and we are responsible for what the  government does which means we also have  to supervise we have to know what the  government does and supervise it and if  it is not morally appropriate what the  government is doing or immoral or wrong  we have to speak out and we have to say  not in our name don't do that and that  is true of course as everybody  understands with regard to Foreign Wars  that our government may be involved in  but it is just as true with regard to  economic rules that our government may  be involved in negotiating with other  countries and here of course it is to be  said that the US government is still by  far the strongest government in the  world in international negotiations and  so it matters a great deal  what negotiating position our government  is taking and we mustn't let that be  influenced  solely by the big corporations which of  course are interested only in their own  bottom line we have to make sure that  our government stands for justice and  stands for the common good of humankind  and defends also the interests of the  poorest human beings who really have  nobody else to speak up for them right  because their own governments are often  corrupt and in any case quite powerless  to intervene meaningfully in  international negotiations okay thank  you very much for being here with us and  sharing your work you're very welcome  Marilyn it was a pleasure thank you for  more information about Professor pogey  and his research please visit our  website at macmillan report Yale edu be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you

