t least out  those people who have absorption  problems those people who work hard  those people who have nutritional  deficits other than energy and those  people who have shorter periods so how  does that happen how how does such a  mistake like that happen well I surmise  and I have a little bit of anecdotal  evidence about that I surmise that this  is because the FAO got under a lot of  pressure they got under pressure  especially when they came out with this  big news more than a billion people  hungry they hung that from the windows  of the FAO headquarters in Rome it was  big news in 2009 and so it got a lot of  governments upset because governments of  course were very proud of their  globalization project and how it was  lifting all boats and helping especially  the poor and we would never engage in it  if it went helping the poor and so on  and so the FAO got under pressure and  felt they needed to do something now why  didn't the FAO hold they head up high  and say these are the numbers and you  better live with them and you have to  change your policies rather than me  change our numbers right well the FAO is  an intergovernmental organization their  leaders are appointed by governments  their budgets are decided by governments  about 58% of the FAO budget comes at  hawk where governments sort of it's not  the regular budget that they get  regularly but it's additional money that  they get for this or that project on an  ad-hoc basis and so it's very important  if you care about poor people and if you  care about the work that the FAO is  doing to be on good terms with  governments and if governments get  really displeased with you right they  will not give you money and so the FAO  people where I think in a very difficult  situation on the one hand they felt that  being honest and true to the poor meant  they had to defend their numbers and say  this is what's really happening in the  world hunger is getting worse on the  other hand if you want to keep up your  good work and get the cooperation of  governments you feel that maybe you have  to betray the numbers and just give the  government's what they want so that they  give you what you need to is like a  catch-22 how do you get around that but  the answer is very obvious we cannot  leave the measurement of progress  to these weak vulnerable politically  exposed intergovernmental agencies  because we put them in an impossible  position what do we have universities  for right we have fantastic experts on  food on hunger on poverty in  universities we have an existing network  of universities that spans the globe of  people who are working together already  who are in the same discipline who  peer-review one another's work we could  easily create a poverty measurement and  the nourishment measurement operation  the network of people that is much  better than anything the FAO can field  and more importantly it would be  independent  these are first-rate academics who have  big reputations to lose and they would  deliver the correct verdict without fear  or favor they would not be easily  manipulated by governments into changing  their tune just to make the numbers look  better is that one of your findings in  your book one of the suggestions that we  make I think it's a very important thing  to press and here you see how for we're  going for the SDGs we have an important  message right let the SDGs be different  let the measurement be taken out of the  hands of the people who are doing the  work let the FAO be the leader on the  work on the ground let them lead the  effort to fight hunger and  undernutrition  but the observation the monitoring of  that fight how it's going let that be  done by an independent objective agency  so that the world really knows that make  sense yes  what are some of your other findings  well it's hard to summarize because  there are a lot of different things but  maybe I say one or two more things that  are interesting for the viewers perhaps  to think about so one of the things that  actually I contributed to the volume is  the thought that if you think about  these Development Goals you are  systematically  thinking in a way that's quite different  from the way we often think about these  matters of deprivation maybe in human  rights terms and so I reflect upon the  difference between the goals language  and the rights language okay the goals  language says basically here is  something that we want to attain in the  future and let's just walk in the right  direction and keep going and sooner or  later we'll get to our goal the rights  language says here are people who are  suffering severe deprivation z-- their  human rights are unfulfilled they have a  right that immediate action be taken to  fulfill their needs so for example under  nutrition or extreme poverty these are  the objects of human rights in the  Universal Declaration in the  international covenants and so on and so  something needs to be done immediately  so in a sense the goal language is  watering down humanity's commitment to  eradicate deprivation and to drive that  home just imagine for example FDR  Roosevelt had said in 1941 you know the  horrible things are being done by the  Nazis they're over running countries in  Europe they are putting hundreds of  thousands of people into concentration  camps we should set the goal of having  the number of people suffering by 1960  in nineteen years or something like that  I mean that would have been a pretty  ridiculous thing to say if people's  human rights are being violated you  can't sort of make that a goal and say  yeah we do a little bit every day and we  say get it down and so I think that  again we were too easily sucked into the  language of goals and thereby appeased  with regard to demanding immediate full  action and you know the thing what is  really heartbreaking about these  problems of deprivation of poverty of  undernourishment of disease is that in  economic terms these problems are tiny  you know at the cost of invading Iraq  you could abolish  hunger pensee  poverty worldwide very easily it is  economically a small problem and in  human terms it's still half of humanity  are living on the brink of  life-threatening deprivation and today  with today's technology is  administrative capacities economic  wherewithal we could wipe these problems  out and I think we should i completely  agree  why do you think we are not wiping them  out since it is possible well I think  that politicians for better or worse  think that this is something that  nobody's really going to reward them for  and to the contrary voters may feel  restive if too much money is spent  abroad you see it for example with the  promise that was made in the 1970s where  all the developed countries promised  they would spend 0.7% of their gross  national product on development  assistance a few countries have reached  that target Britain very recently  Finland Sweden Norway  I think Denmark maybe the Netherlands at  some point but not anymore but the vast  majority of countries has never reached  that goal never gotten anywhere near the  United States is sort of hovering in the  teens even I mean way below 0.7  somewhere around zero point one nine one  eight something like that so this is  simply that it's too easy for  nationalist politicians to step forward  and to say the government is giving away  the store you know they are giving all  your text money to foreigners and then  of course you start talking about the  corruption abroad and so on and that  it's sending money down a rat hole and  so forth and so I think that is the way  that it's just too easy for nationalist  politicians to block and to oppose this  kind of move and frankly the you know  few politicians really care enough  politicians are for the most part  interested in their own careers in their  own success politically and when they  win and when they have power they try to  preserve that power and they don't  really think about the morally most  urgent issues so in terms of the  sustainable development goals that have  been developed what are your thoughts on  those well the sustainable development  goals are very different they are much  much longer so you have instead of 18  targets you have 169 targets Wow the  MDGs were eight goals and 18 targets so  here we have 17 goals in 169 targets so  it's a very very long list and it is a  more comprehensive list which again has  some of the old goals included in it but  also a lot of other new things and one  thing that is especially noteworthy and  important is that you have inequality as  a specific goal that inequality should  be reduced the goal is then watered down  by one of the targets which says that  the reduction of inequality should have  begun before 2030 so inequality can  still go up until 2029 and then it  should which is really lame you know we  should think about inequality right now  and we should certainly certainly aim to  have less inequality in the world both  international and international in 2030  than we have today  so but anyway the SDGs at least have  talked about inequality they talk about  illicit financial flows a very important  topic that a lot of money is being  sucked out of poor countries with the  help of tax havens corrupt law firms  accounting firms financial advisers and  so on important multinational  corporations as in the meantime we all  have learned pay very little taxes in  the u.s. very little in Europe but guess  how much they pay in the poorer  countries who are much more defenseless  and much less able to resist right they  pay no taxes they basically shift all  their profits into tax havens and at  last count the multinational  corporations together have about 2.3  trillion dollars sitting in tax havens  completely untaxed waiting to spend that  money in ways you know but certainly  trying to avoid forever paying taxes  right many companies are now trying to  use inversions where they buy a company  abroad and then shift their headquarters  to the company they've bought so Pfizer  is currently doing that a big  pharmaceutical company trying to become  a non-us company and thereby permanently  to avoid paying taxes on the accumulated  money in tax havens so that's a problem  that the SDGs at least flag they mention  it they don't FEMA ties it in an  appropriate way but at least it's in  there and so and then of course the  environment the name sustainable  development goals already suggests that  there is a concern with the environment  and at least it gets mentioned ok so  ultimately what do you hope your book  will do the book I hope it will educate  citizens because we as citizens have a  responsibility to think about what is  being done in our names right we live in  a democratic society our government acts  in the name of the people of the country  and we are responsible for what the  government does which means we also have  to supervise we have to know what the  government does and supervise it and if  it is not morally appropriate what the  government is doing or immoral or wrong  we have to speak out and we have to say  not in our name don't do that and that  is true of course as everybody  understands with regard to Foreign Wars  that our government may be involved in  but it is just as true with regard to  economic rules that our government may  be involved in negotiating with other  countries and here of course it is to be  said that the US government is still by  far the strongest government in the  world in international negotiations and  so it matters a great deal  what negotiating position our government  is taking and we mustn't let that be  influenced  solely by the big corporations which of  course are interested only in their own  bottom line we have to make sure that  our government stands for justice and  stands for the common good of humankind  and defends also the interests of the  poorest human beings who really have  nobody else to speak up for them right  because their own governments are often  corrupt and in any case quite powerless  to intervene meaningfully in  international negotiations okay thank  you very much for being here with us and  sharing your work you're very welcome  Marilyn it was a pleasure thank you for  more information about Professor pogey  and his research please visit our  website at macmillan report Yale edu be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

